1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,141
By the end of this video, you will know how to pick the right skin suit size and how to check the fit properly.

2
00:00:07,141 --> 00:00:11,709
You will also know how to wash and store it so it lasts a whole season.

3
00:00:11,709 --> 00:00:17,114
The skin suit is the piece of kit skaters think about least and complain about most.

4
00:00:17,114 --> 00:00:21,553
On race day, someone is always tugging at a sleeve on the start line.

5
00:00:21,553 --> 00:00:28,116
Or they find that a suit which felt fine in the shop cuts into their shoulders the moment they crouch.

6
00:00:28,116 --> 00:00:29,467
The reason is simple.

7
00:00:29,467 --> 00:00:31,269
A skin suit is not clothing.

8
00:00:31,269 --> 00:00:34,357
It is equipment, and it is sized like equipment.

9
00:00:35,057 --> 00:00:37,580
Your normal clothes are cut with ease.

10
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:44,751
Ease is the extra room a garment carries beyond your body measurement, so it hangs nicely and lets you move.

11
00:00:44,751 --> 00:00:46,875
A skin suit works the other way.

12
00:00:46,875 --> 00:00:48,270
It has negative ease.

13
00:00:48,270 --> 00:00:51,855
It is cut smaller than you, and it stretches onto you.

14
00:00:51,855 --> 00:00:55,706
The fabric is meant to be under tension while you wear it.

15
00:00:55,706 --> 00:01:00,154
That is why the advice to order your usual size does not help here.

16
00:01:00,154 --> 00:01:03,275
Your usual size assumes there is room to spare.

17
00:01:03,275 --> 00:01:09,317
Almost everything you know about picking a t shirt size works against you with a race suit.

18
00:01:10,017 --> 00:01:12,272
This diagram shows the difference.

19
00:01:12,272 --> 00:01:18,705
On the left, everyday clothes are cut larger than your body, with a gap of spare room all around.

20
00:01:18,705 --> 00:01:22,552
On the right, the race suit is cut smaller than your body.

21
00:01:22,552 --> 00:01:27,593
When you put it on, it stretches and holds itself under tension against you.

22
00:01:27,593 --> 00:01:31,373
There is no gap for air to get into, and nothing to flap.

23
00:01:31,373 --> 00:01:34,888
Keep this picture in mind whenever you try on a suit.

24
00:01:34,888 --> 00:01:38,669
If it feels like normal clothing, it is probably too big.

25
00:01:39,369 --> 00:01:42,674
Here is the single most useful idea in this guide.

26
00:01:42,674 --> 00:01:45,450
Always judge fit in your skating position.

27
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:47,764
The common mistake looks like this.

28
00:01:47,764 --> 00:01:53,647
A skater stands upright in front of a mirror, decides the suit feels tight, and sizes up.

29
00:01:53,647 --> 00:01:59,067
Then they get low, and the suit is loose across the back and bunching at the hips.

30
00:01:59,067 --> 00:02:00,522
The diagram shows why.

31
00:02:00,522 --> 00:02:04,356
A suit that feels comfortable standing is usually too big.

32
00:02:04,356 --> 00:02:10,635
A suit that feels snug standing and comfortable in your low position is the one cut for racing.

33
00:02:10,635 --> 00:02:14,205
Your position is the shape the suit was patterned for.

34
00:02:14,905 --> 00:02:18,244
So put the suit on and get into your real position.

35
00:02:18,244 --> 00:02:24,660
Bend your knees, keep your back flat, chest low, and put your arms where they live when you skate.

36
00:02:24,660 --> 00:02:26,166
Now check three things.

37
00:02:26,166 --> 00:02:31,076
First, can you breathe fully into your ribs at the bottom of your position?

38
00:02:31,076 --> 00:02:33,564
Second, extend one leg fully sideways.

39
00:02:33,564 --> 00:02:36,968
Does the crotch seam stay put, or does it drag down?

40
00:02:36,968 --> 00:02:39,390
Third, swing an arm across your body.

41
00:02:39,390 --> 00:02:41,813
Does the shoulder pull uncomfortably?

42
00:02:41,813 --> 00:02:43,253
Tightness is expected.

43
00:02:43,253 --> 00:02:47,574
Restricted breathing, or a suit that blocks a full stroke, is not.

44
00:02:48,274 --> 00:02:51,624
Some suits come in two size runs on one product.

45
00:02:51,624 --> 00:02:59,791
The LiveLica Pro Skin Suit, for example, comes in sizes 4 to 16 in steps of two, and also in small, medium and large.

46
00:02:59,791 --> 00:03:03,420
The numbers are the junior and youth end of the run.

47
00:03:03,420 --> 00:03:09,842
They step in twos because young bodies change quickly, so a small gap between sizes matters.

48
00:03:09,842 --> 00:03:14,937
If you are buying for a young skater, this is the run you are working in.

49
00:03:14,937 --> 00:03:18,008
Small, medium and large are the adult sizes.

50
00:03:18,008 --> 00:03:23,732
The steps between them are larger, which is the usual trade off in adult race kit.

51
00:03:24,432 --> 00:03:28,159
Every parent of a junior skater asks this at some point.

52
00:03:28,159 --> 00:03:31,487
Should you buy a size up so the suit lasts longer?

53
00:03:31,487 --> 00:03:36,480
It is an understandable instinct, but it is the wrong call for a race suit.

54
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:39,675
An oversized skin suit does not just look loose.

55
00:03:39,675 --> 00:03:42,271
It stops doing the job it exists to do.

56
00:03:42,271 --> 00:03:44,600
The compression is not compressing.

57
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:48,994
The aerodynamic pattern does not sit where it was designed to sit.

58
00:03:48,994 --> 00:03:52,788
And the leg grippers cannot grip a leg they do not reach.

59
00:03:52,788 --> 00:03:58,179
A suit bought a size up does nothing useful for a year, then fits for six months.

60
00:03:58,179 --> 00:04:00,642
Buy for the body that is in the room.

61
00:04:01,342 --> 00:04:06,438
Fabric weight is measured in grams, and it quietly decides how a suit behaves.

62
00:04:06,438 --> 00:04:09,313
Lighter fabric is cooler and more forgiving.

63
00:04:09,313 --> 00:04:17,740
Heavier fabric holds its shape under load, does not turn see through when stretched in position, and lasts longer before it sags.

64
00:04:17,740 --> 00:04:21,333
A good suit uses different weights in different places.

65
00:04:21,333 --> 00:04:27,932
Look for stretch in two directions, because your leg extends sideways and backwards at the same time.

66
00:04:27,932 --> 00:04:31,786
Fabric that only stretches one way fights you in the other.

67
00:04:31,786 --> 00:04:33,224
And watch for pilling.

68
00:04:33,224 --> 00:04:40,606
Those little balls of fluff are broken fibres, which means the suit is losing compression and its smooth surface.

69
00:04:41,306 --> 00:04:43,491
A few details matter on race day.

70
00:04:43,491 --> 00:04:46,802
Silicone leg grippers stop the suit leg riding up.

71
00:04:46,802 --> 00:04:54,351
Every centimetre it climbs is fabric bunching at your hip, and that bunch creates drag in exactly the wrong place.

72
00:04:54,351 --> 00:04:59,582
A laser cut leg edge means no hem, so there is no seam ridge across your thigh.

73
00:04:59,582 --> 00:05:04,880
Ribbed sleeves are an aerodynamic choice, so sleeves should fit snug, not loose.

74
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:10,905
The zip sits at the back to keep the front of the suit smooth where the air hits you first.

75
00:05:10,905 --> 00:05:13,951
You may need help getting in and out at first.

76
00:05:13,951 --> 00:05:14,945
That is normal.

77
00:05:14,945 --> 00:05:23,950
Do the zip up while the suit sits relaxed on your shoulders, never while it is stretched, and close it before the suit goes in your bag.

78
00:05:24,650 --> 00:05:26,279
Lycra has three enemies.

79
00:05:26,279 --> 00:05:28,925
Heat, chlorine, and time under tension.

80
00:05:28,925 --> 00:05:30,553
You control two of them.

81
00:05:30,553 --> 00:05:34,895
Rinse the suit in cold water as soon as you can after a session.

82
00:05:34,895 --> 00:05:39,780
Dried sweat leaves salt, and salt slowly grinds the stretch fibres down.

83
00:05:39,780 --> 00:05:43,512
Wash it cold, inside out, on a gentle cycle or by hand.

84
00:05:43,512 --> 00:05:49,618
Never use fabric softener, because it coats the fibres and ruins both wicking and stretch.

85
00:05:49,618 --> 00:05:52,129
Never put the suit in a tumble dryer.

86
00:05:52,129 --> 00:05:57,081
Heat kills the elastic fibres, and the suit will never recover its shape.

87
00:05:57,081 --> 00:06:01,017
Dry it flat or on a hanger in the shade, out of harsh sun.

88
00:06:01,017 --> 00:06:07,462
Then store it flat or loosely folded, not stretched over a hanger or balled up in your kit bag.

89
00:06:08,162 --> 00:06:09,458
A few quick answers.

90
00:06:09,458 --> 00:06:12,504
If you are between sizes, generally go smaller.

91
00:06:12,504 --> 00:06:17,753
A suit that is slightly small still works, and one that is slightly big does not.

92
00:06:17,753 --> 00:06:19,503
The exception is breathing.

93
00:06:19,503 --> 00:06:24,039
If the smaller size stops you filling your lungs in position, size up.

94
00:06:24,039 --> 00:06:29,936
Numbness, pins and needles, or a crotch seam that will not stay put are signs of too tight.

95
00:06:29,936 --> 00:06:35,963
Marks on your skin after a session are normal, but not if they are still there an hour later.

96
00:06:35,963 --> 00:06:40,304
Under the suit, wear as little as possible, and nothing with seams.

97
00:06:40,304 --> 00:06:45,942
If your suit is pilling, look at heat, hot washing, or washing it with zips and velcro.

98
00:06:46,642 --> 00:06:49,289
Be realistic about what a suit gives you.

99
00:06:49,289 --> 00:06:51,678
It will not add power to your stroke.

100
00:06:51,678 --> 00:06:56,972
It stops you losing things, like flapping fabric, shifting kit, chafing and sweat.

101
00:06:56,972 --> 00:07:00,330
If you take one thing from this video, make it this.

102
00:07:00,330 --> 00:07:05,366
Try the suit on and get into your skating position before you decide anything.

103
00:07:05,366 --> 00:07:08,659
The mirror lies, but your position tells the truth.

104
00:07:08,659 --> 00:07:17,181
Get the size right, wash it cold, keep it away from the dryer, and a good suit will still be doing its job at the end of the season.

105
00:07:17,181 --> 00:07:22,023
The full guide is on the LiveLica blog, and the link is in the description.
